      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Mount Vernon became Franklin County’s seat in 1875 after the county split from Titus County, securing its role as the region’s economic hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Vernon%2C_Texas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In the 1920s, over 500,000 peach trees were grown in Mount Vernon, making it a leading peach producer in Texas.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Vernon%2C_Texas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town’s name evolved from “Keith” and “Lone Star” to “Mount Vernon” in 1875, reflecting its settlers’ desire for a distinct identity.",
